WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.971 --> 00:00:17.024
So this is going to be the first of four
lectures on how to build a great product.

00:00:17.024 --> 00:00:20.335
I apologize because none of the people
here today can speak to that topic.

00:00:20.335 --> 00:00:23.570
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] My name is Michael.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:29.290
I'm the CEO of YC and
help run the batch and

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.690
program as most of you
understand YC to be.

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:36.720
This here is Steve who's
the co-founder and CEO of Reddit.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.851
And there's Emmet who's cofounder and
CEO of Twitch.

00:00:41.851 --> 00:00:46.454
Can you guys introduce yourselves
in a couple of seconds?

00:00:46.454 --> 00:00:48.695
&gt;&gt; You just did but I can do it again.

00:00:48.695 --> 00:00:52.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And Steve, yeah co-founder and

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.129
CEO of Reddit.

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:55.747
I study computer science.

00:00:55.747 --> 00:00:59.020
I graduated from UVA in 2005.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.865
I was at Reddit for five years,
I left for five years.

00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:06.923
I've been back for
about a year and a half.

00:01:06.923 --> 00:01:12.895
&gt;&gt; My name is Emmet I graduated
from Yale computer science in 2005.

00:01:12.895 --> 00:01:17.464
I started my first company
right out of college.

00:01:17.464 --> 00:01:21.122
We made a bad copy of Google calendar
before Google calendar existed.

00:01:21.122 --> 00:01:23.679
And-
&gt;&gt; Google calendar

00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:25.881
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's right, and

00:01:25.881 --> 00:01:30.860
then we started Justin TV which
is the company that one turn and

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:35.364
so I've been doing,
basically continuously employed

00:01:35.364 --> 00:01:40.102
working on that since 2006
&gt;&gt; And

00:01:40.102 --> 00:01:44.826
then can you just, each of you guys say
whatever the KPI you guys track for

00:01:44.826 --> 00:01:47.709
the business and approximately what it is?

00:01:47.709 --> 00:01:51.557
Just to give folks a sense
of why you guys might be

00:01:51.557 --> 00:01:54.649
experts at building great products.

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:58.397
&gt;&gt; [COUGH] One of our more shareable
ones is monthly active users and

00:01:58.397 --> 00:02:00.457
we hit 300 million last month.

00:02:00.457 --> 00:02:00.971
&gt;&gt; Nice.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:00.971 --> 00:02:03.691
&gt;&gt; That's a good number.

00:02:03.691 --> 00:02:07.877
&gt;&gt; Our primary metric that we care about
is a metric called minutes watched,

00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:11.939
the number of minutes of video that
are consumed every month on Twitch.

00:02:11.939 --> 00:02:16.418
And we just crossed 30 billion minutes.

00:02:16.418 --> 00:02:18.597
This year for the first timers.

00:02:18.597 --> 00:02:19.183
This is very exciting.

00:02:19.183 --> 00:02:21.105
&gt;&gt; Total?
&gt;&gt; No, per month.

00:02:21.105 --> 00:02:22.284
&gt;&gt; Jesus.

00:02:22.284 --> 00:02:23.239
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; So

00:02:23.239 --> 00:02:27.507
that's the main thing we
look at rather than uniques.

00:02:27.507 --> 00:02:29.547
&gt;&gt; So, what we're going to do for

00:02:29.547 --> 00:02:34.649
you guys today is I have about 12
questions I'm going to ask these guys.

00:02:34.649 --> 00:02:37.104
We're going to see how many
of them we get through.

00:02:37.104 --> 00:02:39.984
And then we're going to leave
the last ten minutes for

00:02:39.984 --> 00:02:44.760
questions about start up stuff, YC, or
whatever else you'd like to talk about.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.030
My goal is to kind of focus this
advice on early, early stage.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.575
So, these guys do a lot
of stuff now that really

00:02:52.575 --> 00:02:56.376
is completely irrelevant to you
&gt;&gt; But hopefully they'll be talking to you

00:02:56.376 --> 00:03:00.210
about the things they did, bad or
good, when they were starting.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.810
So, I want to start with
one of the major tenants of

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:06.770
YC is how you talking to your users.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:10.910
And we repeat that a bunch of times, and
then we don't really go into any detail.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:15.060
So, can you guys talk a little bit about
how you talk to talk to your users?

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.685
Maybe the good and maybe some of
the mistakes you've made as well.

00:03:18.685 --> 00:03:23.580
&gt;&gt; Sure, so, I, most of the advice I
give today will have two perspectives,

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.771
one at Reddit and one at Hipmunk.

00:03:25.771 --> 00:03:29.296
because the companies are completely
different and had different paths and

00:03:29.296 --> 00:03:32.055
I think, kind of the ground rules for
this discussion,

00:03:32.055 --> 00:03:35.812
the first piece of advice I would give
is every company is really different,

00:03:35.812 --> 00:03:38.360
and there are lots of
different paths you can take.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:43.360
And what works in one place,
very likely won't work in another.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.790
So at Reddit, I mean the product
is talking to users, right?

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.432
That was built in.

00:03:51.432 --> 00:03:54.930
In the early days we didn't have
comments and we didn't have communities.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.470
So actually,
we got a lot of emails, was our,

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.890
for the first probably six months was
the way we communicated to our users.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.080
But when we added the comment feature,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.870
there was basically no option but
to talk to our users.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:11.980
And the challenge then becomes which
users do you want to listen to?

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:13.500
And what are they actually saying?

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.630
And what do they actually mean?

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:18.470
Because they're often saying one thing and
they mean something else.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:23.840
At Hipmunk, because it was selling
plane tickets and hotel reservations.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.006
It was completely different.

00:04:26.006 --> 00:04:29.130
We didn't really have a good forum for
connecting with our users.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:33.426
We used a product called Olark which
was basically this imbedded Java Script

00:04:33.426 --> 00:04:34.172
chat thing.

00:04:34.172 --> 00:04:39.928
And that was indispensable,
we still use it.

00:04:39.928 --> 00:04:42.800
It was users, if they got stuck,
they could ask us for help.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.900
If they wanted to give us compliments or
criticisms, they could do that.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:50.915
And we can often, both over email at
Reddit and over Olark at Hipmunk,

00:04:50.915 --> 00:04:56.185
turn Like emotional users, angry users
will often become your most loyal

00:04:56.185 --> 00:05:02.069
users if you can flip them around, there's
like this absolute value of emotion.

00:05:02.069 --> 00:05:05.375
And so, if you can find those angry
users that are having a bad time and

00:05:05.375 --> 00:05:07.075
treat them well they will likely.

00:05:07.075 --> 00:05:08.915
Quickly turn into some of
your most loyal supporters.

00:05:08.915 --> 00:05:10.829
So, we found that whole
cycle to be very valuable.

00:05:12.465 --> 00:05:15.898
&gt;&gt; The interesting thing for me,
between Twitch and Justin TV,

00:05:15.898 --> 00:05:18.615
which are two very similar
product right there.

00:05:18.615 --> 00:05:20.465
With live video products with chat.

00:05:20.465 --> 00:05:22.795
So for Justin TV, we were our own users.

00:05:22.795 --> 00:05:25.785
We were building a product for
us to build a TV show.

00:05:25.785 --> 00:05:26.767
And after that,

00:05:26.767 --> 00:05:31.338
we really sort of declined to actually
speak to users in any significant way.

00:05:31.338 --> 00:05:35.479
And, unsurprisingly, all of the good
ideas we had were things we came up with

00:05:35.479 --> 00:05:37.659
when we were building it for ourselves.

00:05:37.659 --> 00:05:41.100
Which sort of brings me to my first point,
of talking to users, is it's possible,

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.380
especially very early,
to build a product yourself.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:47.130
And, if you truly are building
something that is for

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:49.590
you to use, that can be super effective.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.050
You don't necessarily need to
talk to talk to anyone else.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:56.193
Eventually, you'll need to, because you'll
learn about people other than yourself but

00:05:56.193 --> 00:06:00.521
for the first three months it's to develop
something, just something that you want.

00:06:00.521 --> 00:06:02.430
But you have to actually really want it.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.564
It can't be, you can't be like
lying to yourself about that,

00:06:04.564 --> 00:06:05.993
it can't be something you think is cool.

00:06:05.993 --> 00:06:11.613
It has to be something you, yourself
actually desperately want to use everyday.

00:06:11.613 --> 00:06:14.371
And then we had went through a long
period in the desert where we were

00:06:14.371 --> 00:06:15.810
wondering about.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:19.056
Not talking to users,
not really using our own product, and

00:06:19.056 --> 00:06:20.850
not building anything of value.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:23.683
And the thing we did with Twitch
that I thought was really good for

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:25.850
production users is we
talked to them in person.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:30.590
I had done some amount of surveys and
talking to users over email.

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:34.835
And I found that getting people on,
at least on Skype, or if not,

00:06:34.835 --> 00:06:39.566
literally in person changed my depth
of understanding of who they were.

00:06:39.566 --> 00:06:43.856
The other thing I would say about about
it is that we focused down really hard on

00:06:43.856 --> 00:06:45.780
which of our users we cared about.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:47.710
And we decided it was the broadcasters and

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:51.280
not only was it the broadcasters,
it was the successful broadcasters.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:53.200
We didn't want to talk to
people who had two viewers.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.738
We wanted to talk to
people who had 200 views.

00:06:55.738 --> 00:06:59.321
And so, we went and talked to a bunch
of broadcasters on the platform who

00:06:59.321 --> 00:07:01.310
decided they were the important ones.

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:05.122
The other thing we did that was I think
really valuable is we decided it wasn't

00:07:05.122 --> 00:07:09.112
just people who were currently using our
service it was people who we wanted to use

00:07:09.112 --> 00:07:09.834
our service.

00:07:09.834 --> 00:07:14.407
And so, we talked to a bunch of people who
were streaming on other platforms rather

00:07:14.407 --> 00:07:16.078
than just already on Twitch.

00:07:16.078 --> 00:07:19.932
Because the people who have
chosen to not use your product,

00:07:19.932 --> 00:07:24.018
especially if they have deliberately
looked at your product,

00:07:24.018 --> 00:07:27.563
decided it sucked and
went somewhere else, are like,

00:07:27.563 --> 00:07:32.515
some of the best people to talk to,
because they know what's wrong with it.

00:07:32.515 --> 00:07:36.900
And so, I actually think the most valuable
things I learned, especially early on,

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:40.319
were from users who had tried Justin TV,
they had tried UStream.

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:43.234
They tried own 3D, they tried YouTube, and

00:07:43.234 --> 00:07:47.620
they had a coherent opinion as to
why ours was not the one they chose.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:49.135
And that was super valuable,

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:53.370
because they really pointed at
the details of where we were wrong.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.250
&gt;&gt; And often those details
are smaller than you think.

00:07:57.647 --> 00:08:02.241
&gt;&gt; It's like memorably one of
the biggest issues was we were

00:08:02.241 --> 00:08:06.557
not listed on
the Team Liquid Starcraft fan website.

00:08:06.557 --> 00:08:11.496
We lost maybe 3,000
broadcasters over that.

00:08:11.496 --> 00:08:14.249
We had to email them and
ask them to re list us,

00:08:14.249 --> 00:08:16.793
because they just didn't have us there.

00:08:16.793 --> 00:08:20.187
Tiny issue, but
like really mattered to our users.

00:08:20.187 --> 00:08:24.281
&gt;&gt; It's easy to run a company for
years and then be like,

00:08:24.281 --> 00:08:29.562
I should have fixed this stupid one day
fix if you don't talk to your users.

00:08:29.562 --> 00:08:34.112
&gt;&gt; So a lot of people talk about MVPs,
minimum viable products.

00:08:34.112 --> 00:08:37.293
How many of you have heard that term,
MVPs?

00:08:37.293 --> 00:08:41.126
So one of the challenges I have is
when folks like you apply to YC and

00:08:41.126 --> 00:08:45.731
get into YC even though you know the idea
that you should be releasing something

00:08:45.731 --> 00:08:47.777
that is crappy, you don't do it.

00:08:47.777 --> 00:08:51.581
And as a result, users slow down.

00:08:51.581 --> 00:08:55.416
And oftentimes,
that prevents you from getting into YC or

00:08:55.416 --> 00:08:59.027
prevents you from building and
talking to customers.

00:08:59.027 --> 00:09:02.713
And so, I think that what's
interesting is that just TV and

00:09:02.713 --> 00:09:05.900
Reddit are pretty good
examples of crappy MVP's.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:11.012
So, can you guys describe how
the products worked in the beginning or

00:09:11.012 --> 00:09:13.669
maybe how they only kind of worked?

00:09:13.669 --> 00:09:19.875
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we started, I think I wrote
my first line of code on June 3rd or

00:09:19.875 --> 00:09:25.226
4th, 2005 for Reddit and
we launched on June 22nd.

00:09:25.226 --> 00:09:26.558
I didn't even launch it.

00:09:26.558 --> 00:09:29.664
Paul Graham linked to us from
his blog without telling me.

00:09:29.664 --> 00:09:32.308
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And from then,

00:09:32.308 --> 00:09:35.412
it was on and
the good thing about that for

00:09:35.412 --> 00:09:40.525
us in particular is we didn't
actually have a vision for Reddit for

00:09:40.525 --> 00:09:46.858
better or for worse which would affect us
in a lot of different ways going forward.

00:09:46.858 --> 00:09:51.035
But as soon as we launched, that's when we
started to find the path and we just would

00:09:51.035 --> 00:09:54.746
kind of follow the users everyday as
what do we think is best for the users?

00:09:54.746 --> 00:09:56.708
What do we think is best for us?

00:09:56.708 --> 00:09:58.600
And just build towards that and

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:01.948
we've built a lot of loyalty
through those actions and

00:10:01.948 --> 00:10:06.985
that would not have happened, if we were
sitting in our apartment not launching.

00:10:06.985 --> 00:10:11.959
Because if we launched and then for the
next six months, we just added a ton of

00:10:11.959 --> 00:10:16.856
new features, only about 25% of which
lasted more than a day or two, but

00:10:16.856 --> 00:10:19.695
there was stuff we
though was a great idea.

00:10:19.695 --> 00:10:23.309
Like we had these categories,
I remember in July at some point.

00:10:23.309 --> 00:10:26.527
I made Alexis my co-founder stay up all
night one night categorizing every link

00:10:26.527 --> 00:10:28.171
that'd ever been submitted to Reddit.

00:10:28.171 --> 00:10:30.198
And the next morning, I was like we lost.

00:10:30.198 --> 00:10:35.022
It's not working turn it off, but
we could've probably, if we had launched,

00:10:35.022 --> 00:10:39.054
we could have had that feature until
November and then launched and

00:10:39.054 --> 00:10:41.220
then wondered why it didn't work.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:46.279
&gt;&gt; I think that Amazon actually has
a really good saying about mineral and

00:10:46.279 --> 00:10:47.573
viral products.

00:10:47.573 --> 00:10:50.123
They like to call them,
what they're aiming for

00:10:50.123 --> 00:10:54.164
a minimum remarkable product which I
think gets more at what you're trying to

00:10:54.164 --> 00:10:58.517
accomplish which is a viral product bring
into mind this whole ecosystem of support

00:10:58.517 --> 00:11:02.059
like a viable organism that's got
all the things it needs to live and

00:11:02.059 --> 00:11:03.763
you don't actually want that.

00:11:03.763 --> 00:11:08.108
Like if you're launching something that's
actually self-sufficient, that's viable in

00:11:08.108 --> 00:11:11.523
a this thing could live on its own sense,
you definitely waited too long.

00:11:11.523 --> 00:11:13.512
This thing is going to be on live
support when you launch it and

00:11:13.512 --> 00:11:14.660
you are the life support system.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:18.714
What you actually want is
you want something about

00:11:18.714 --> 00:11:22.876
the product when you launch
it to be actually good.

00:11:22.876 --> 00:11:27.260
You want to get to the part where you've
built something that maybe at least one

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:31.645
person in the world finds remarkably
useful or remarkably good and you don't

00:11:31.645 --> 00:11:35.761
know really if you've gotten there
until you put it in front of someone,

00:11:35.761 --> 00:11:38.351
which is why you need to launch it so
quickly.

00:11:38.351 --> 00:11:41.677
I think it's really important to
remember Dropbox, for example,

00:11:41.677 --> 00:11:45.702
one of the big YC successes did not launch
an immediate product, because they were

00:11:45.702 --> 00:11:49.636
building a backup system and backup
systems aren't allowed to lose your data.

00:11:49.636 --> 00:11:53.532
[LAUGH] And so, the last thing you want to
do if you're building backup software is

00:11:53.532 --> 00:11:56.732
go rush something out that's
going to lose your customer's data,

00:11:56.732 --> 00:11:58.900
because you will never
get that trust back.

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:06.070
And so, the minimum remarkable product for
Dropbox was a video.

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:10.198
Basically, saying, look at this awesome
thing we were going to build and

00:12:10.198 --> 00:12:14.594
getting all the senate for a newsletter
and that worked, because the idea like

00:12:14.594 --> 00:12:18.830
the visual idea of what Dropbox is
going to do was super compelling.

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:22.052
That would not have worked for just in TV.

00:12:22.052 --> 00:12:25.081
We really need to get something out
in front of customers Immediately.

00:12:25.081 --> 00:12:29.285
Most importantly, because we all need to
push the show in this idea we are going to

00:12:29.285 --> 00:12:32.535
build the show and
we basically did the minimum possible work

00:12:32.535 --> 00:12:35.800
may be a little less than that
to get our show on the internet.

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:41.940
And when we launch it,
it like could only have one channel.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:48.755
The way that broadcasting worked,
the only way we could

00:12:48.755 --> 00:12:53.643
actually get it to work
consistently is we had a Mac Mini

00:12:53.643 --> 00:12:59.171
running Parallels which is
a virtualization environment for

00:12:59.171 --> 00:13:03.739
Windows XP where we had this
piece of a broadcasting

00:13:03.739 --> 00:13:08.444
software called,
it had got bought by Adobe later.

00:13:08.444 --> 00:13:09.524
Onflicks?

00:13:09.524 --> 00:13:10.475
OnLot?

00:13:10.475 --> 00:13:11.260
I can't remember.

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:16.543
Anyway, that we were automating
with Windows shell scripting

00:13:16.543 --> 00:13:22.323
to pick up the right camera input and
then auto reboot if it went down,

00:13:22.323 --> 00:13:28.104
because it broke all the time because
it was really buggy software and

00:13:28.104 --> 00:13:31.920
that extremely jury-rigged thing worked.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:35.501
It was like put a video stream on
the internet and it was not a scale.

00:13:35.501 --> 00:13:38.119
We could not scale that up
to thousands of streams.

00:13:38.119 --> 00:13:43.933
It was impossible, but we got something
that at least was compelling to one person

00:13:43.933 --> 00:13:49.680
onto the internet, that was compelling to
us and that was a critical step for us.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:50.400
because before, we did that.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:53.384
We basically weren't learning anything.

00:13:53.384 --> 00:13:57.637
&gt;&gt; At HitMonk, we again,
started in June 2010 and

00:13:57.637 --> 00:14:03.740
I'd given my co-founder, basically
yhis ultimatum of what our MVP was.

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:08.352
We have to have legit data,
because we could scrape anybody.

00:14:08.352 --> 00:14:11.265
We needed legit data, real data.

00:14:11.265 --> 00:14:15.833
And when a customer buys a flight,
we get revenue.

00:14:15.833 --> 00:14:17.514
And I'll say,
if we can do that in three months,

00:14:17.514 --> 00:14:18.830
we will continue with this business.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:21.900
And if we can't launch that in
three months, we're calling it.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:27.227
And that for me, that MVP,
that was that minimum useful thing.

00:14:27.227 --> 00:14:29.072
Like if we had a customer
who was giving us money,

00:14:29.072 --> 00:14:31.130
that meant we did something right.

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:33.648
And unfortunately, we actually
achieved that in three months, so

00:14:33.648 --> 00:14:34.762
I worked on it for five years.

00:14:34.762 --> 00:14:36.942
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:14:36.942 --> 00:14:40.366
I think it's good, sometimes to set
yourself deadlines and specific

00:14:40.366 --> 00:14:44.090
milestones to keep you focused and
keep you pointed in the right direction.

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:49.746
&gt;&gt; So another thing that comes up
a lot and this is actually one of

00:14:49.746 --> 00:14:54.501
the things that hits the, when a company
gets into YC is we will often ask them,

00:14:54.501 --> 00:14:56.688
what are they doing for analytics?

00:14:56.688 --> 00:14:58.720
What are they tracking?

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:02.140
And I think that this is another one of
those MVP like things, everyone knows they

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:06.390
should be doing it and it's the first
thing that gets caught off of any list.

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:09.502
So, can you guys talk about
how an early stage startup

00:15:09.502 --> 00:15:11.381
should think about measuring?

00:15:11.381 --> 00:15:13.245
What analytics tools should they be using?

00:15:13.245 --> 00:15:18.050
And generally, like how you incorporate
tracking into building a product?

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:23.554
&gt;&gt; I have two very different answers,
so reality is somewhere in between.

00:15:23.554 --> 00:15:25.053
At Reddit, we just didn't.

00:15:25.053 --> 00:15:26.841
We didn't know our traffic for years.

00:15:26.841 --> 00:15:28.000
We didn't measure anything.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.430
We did the product development by
intuition for a very long time.

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:35.036
The company ended up working out.

00:15:35.036 --> 00:15:39.163
I'm still now quite sure how many
users we have and it's a huge,

00:15:39.163 --> 00:15:40.780
huge pain in the **** now.

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:43.700
&gt;&gt; How many users you have?

00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:48.288
When you get to 300 million, it's like-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:15:48.288 --> 00:15:48.983
&gt;&gt; No, in all seriousness,

00:15:48.983 --> 00:15:50.664
we have no idea how many unique
people use the site every month.

00:15:50.664 --> 00:15:52.770
&gt;&gt; It's probably somewhere between 250 and
320.

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:54.132
Yeah.

00:15:54.132 --> 00:15:57.684
&gt;&gt; Anyway, at Hipmunk we were actually
really good about it from day one.

00:15:57.684 --> 00:16:01.267
And we were really because we didn't
have that kind of instant feedback with

00:16:01.267 --> 00:16:04.964
the users we actually got really
disciplined about testing, and analytics,

00:16:04.964 --> 00:16:06.481
and measuring, and watching.

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:09.240
And that company lasted honestly,

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:12.330
quite a bit longer than I think
it would have without doing that.

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:13.936
And so if I can do it again,

00:16:13.936 --> 00:16:18.172
there are a lot of corners you can
cut when you're building your MVP.

00:16:18.172 --> 00:16:22.859
You can create all kinds of tech sins and
corporate sins and I don't know,

00:16:22.859 --> 00:16:26.504
be completely dysfunctional,
your users don't care.

00:16:26.504 --> 00:16:29.526
But data debt can haunt you.

00:16:29.526 --> 00:16:32.485
because you can't make up missing data,
and

00:16:32.485 --> 00:16:35.976
eventually your intuition's
going to fail you.

00:16:35.976 --> 00:16:40.042
So I would think very carefully about
what's the minimum viable data.

00:16:40.042 --> 00:16:41.508
You don't even have to look at it.

00:16:41.508 --> 00:16:44.661
Just log it somewhere so
it's there when you need it.

00:16:44.661 --> 00:16:48.375
&gt;&gt; To reinforce that point,
having historical baselines for

00:16:48.375 --> 00:16:52.620
important user actions,
you will thank yourself later.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:56.860
Every time I've ever been like, right,
how many people are using that feature?

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:01.010
And we don't know, and we can't find out.

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:04.852
And now even if we find out how much it is
now, we don't know if it's up a lot, or

00:17:04.852 --> 00:17:05.454
down a lot.

00:17:05.454 --> 00:17:08.250
And we have no idea what the trends are.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.850
It's awful and
you can't make a decision for

00:17:10.850 --> 00:17:14.080
another three to six months
as you build that baseline.

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:17.708
Actually I got the best advice about
this from Sue Hale, who runs Mixpanel,

00:17:17.708 --> 00:17:20.607
when he was convincing us to run
Mixpanel for the first time.

00:17:20.607 --> 00:17:24.614
Which, by the way, didn't work, because
Mixpanel at the time was only six month

00:17:24.614 --> 00:17:27.360
old, and
it broke when we tried to everything.

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:28.415
But, we went back to it-
&gt;&gt; Thanks for

00:17:28.415 --> 00:17:30.651
convincing me to use Mixpanel
before you came to that conclusion.

00:17:30.651 --> 00:17:32.109
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You're welcome.

00:17:32.109 --> 00:17:33.047
&gt;&gt; It works better now.

00:17:33.047 --> 00:17:38.209
&gt;&gt; It works a lot better now, we went back
to it, we're still on Mixpanel today.

00:17:38.209 --> 00:17:42.065
We use a bunch of other stuff to, because
we're a little too big for just Mixpanel.

00:17:42.065 --> 00:17:46.309
But Mixpanel,
the advice Sue Hale gave me that was,

00:17:46.309 --> 00:17:51.041
I think, more important that
the software we were using.

00:17:51.041 --> 00:17:55.001
Mixpanel in specific, even though
I do think it's good software was,

00:17:55.001 --> 00:17:59.555
pick your top five to seven most important
user actions, whatever those are, and

00:17:59.555 --> 00:18:01.300
just log those.

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:03.620
You don't need to log everything,
you really don't.

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:08.000
Most of the things people are going to
do with your product aren't important.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:10.241
There's maybe five things people can do.

00:18:10.241 --> 00:18:14.338
Like for Reddit, Reddit might be vote
on something, comment on something,

00:18:14.338 --> 00:18:18.069
submit a story, load a page,
I don't know some short list of things.

00:18:18.069 --> 00:18:22.381
And those are important and you can
kind of just ignore everything else.

00:18:22.381 --> 00:18:24.989
If they're not instrumented who cares.

00:18:24.989 --> 00:18:30.619
And so we instrumented,
I watched a minute of video,

00:18:30.619 --> 00:18:35.247
I sent a chat message,
I followed a stream,

00:18:35.247 --> 00:18:38.388
and I bought a subscription.

00:18:38.388 --> 00:18:41.888
And that turned out to just give, those
four actions gave us a huge amount of

00:18:41.888 --> 00:18:45.628
insight into what was going on with our
customers, that we hadn't had before.

00:18:45.628 --> 00:18:49.120
&gt;&gt; This is one of those areas where, if I
could go back and give myself just advice

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:52.885
for 30 minutes, that would dramatically
affect the trajectory of the company,

00:18:52.885 --> 00:18:54.308
it would be around this topic.

00:18:54.308 --> 00:18:58.527
And so if I were in your shoes,
I would just take half a day, and

00:18:58.527 --> 00:19:03.091
just read best practices for
collecting events and storing them.

00:19:03.091 --> 00:19:06.353
&gt;&gt; But and also, I know another thing I'd
recommend is use a third party service.

00:19:06.353 --> 00:19:10.582
Use, collect logs, by all means,
put them into your own system.

00:19:10.582 --> 00:19:11.393
because I guarantee you,

00:19:11.393 --> 00:19:14.502
you'll want to do something a third party
service doesn't allow at some point and

00:19:14.502 --> 00:19:17.670
you'll be super annoyed if your data's
only in the third party service.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:20.390
So collect logs or whatever,
but just in the beginning,

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:23.940
you don't have time to mess around
with building analytics tools.

00:19:23.940 --> 00:19:27.480
So just use Mixpanel or
use Google Analytics or use whatever.

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:31.652
Anything, they're all better
than what you need right now.

00:19:31.652 --> 00:19:33.370
Later on you'll need
something more complicated.

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:33.930
You don't need it today.

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:41.080
&gt;&gt; So one of the challenges that
happens usually after a company,

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:45.499
in the early stages,
starts talking to their customers.

00:19:45.499 --> 00:19:50.168
Is they realize maybe there's a big
disconnect between what they thought their

00:19:50.168 --> 00:19:52.967
customers wanted and
what they actually want.

00:19:52.967 --> 00:19:57.202
And at YC this usually comes to
us in office hours in terms of,

00:19:57.202 --> 00:19:59.040
we call the big redesign.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:03.769
So everything about our site's incorrect.

00:20:03.769 --> 00:20:07.150
We just basically need to
rebuild it from scratch.

00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:12.089
Or rebuild huge components of it from
scratch in order to start serving our

00:20:12.089 --> 00:20:12.972
customers.

00:20:12.972 --> 00:20:18.148
Needless to say, this is a very,
how do I phrase this?

00:20:18.148 --> 00:20:21.963
Distracting and
oftentimes not fruitful exercise.

00:20:21.963 --> 00:20:26.699
But how do you see that type of challenge
of, man, we got this feedback and

00:20:26.699 --> 00:20:30.251
we think we need to be going
in a different direction now?

00:20:30.251 --> 00:20:33.565
But how do you deal with
that kind of challenge?

00:20:35.505 --> 00:20:38.134
&gt;&gt; That's a big one.

00:20:38.134 --> 00:20:42.644
And I think you need to be clear whether
you're talking about tech or the product.

00:20:42.644 --> 00:20:47.401
I'll repeat what I said before,
is your users don't care about your tech.

00:20:47.401 --> 00:20:50.768
So if you can move forward,
leave that for another day.

00:20:50.768 --> 00:20:55.776
When you're talking about
redesigning your products.

00:20:55.776 --> 00:20:58.067
You're probably in trouble,

00:20:58.067 --> 00:21:03.977
because whatever habits led you to build
a product your customers don't like.

00:21:03.977 --> 00:21:06.560
If you haven't addressed those habits,

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:11.293
you're probably going to build another
product your customers don't like.

00:21:11.293 --> 00:21:14.839
So I would make sure that, because
I've been through lots of rewrites.

00:21:14.839 --> 00:21:19.390
At Hipmunk we rewrote out flight and
hotels probably half a dozen times each.

00:21:19.390 --> 00:21:21.218
At Reddit,
we re-wrote the tech a bunch of times.

00:21:21.218 --> 00:21:24.412
And we're redoing the UI and

00:21:24.412 --> 00:21:29.211
UX now with better habits and better data.

00:21:29.211 --> 00:21:33.798
And so you want to make sure
that you know something new.

00:21:33.798 --> 00:21:38.703
But honestly, if you're in that situation,
I would also go through

00:21:38.703 --> 00:21:43.284
another mental exercise of okay,
what's the new MVP, right?

00:21:43.284 --> 00:21:46.090
It comes up in tech a lot, right?

00:21:46.090 --> 00:21:47.650
The second system syndrome.

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:49.520
The same thing happens in the product
where you're like, all right,

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:50.940
I've got this vision.

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:54.030
I'm going to fix all of the products,
all of the mistakes at once, and

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:55.290
it's going to be perfect.

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:58.020
That product never ships.

00:21:58.020 --> 00:21:59.550
It never ships.

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:02.550
So can you iterate your way there, can you
just start from scratch with an MVP and

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:03.150
build it back up?

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:05.378
Those are much more likely to
actually get you somewhere.

00:22:05.378 --> 00:22:09.224
But they're also harder to do, and
requires a little bit more discipline.

00:22:09.224 --> 00:22:12.380
&gt;&gt; So we've also gone
through a bunch of redesigns.

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:17.090
Big redesigns where you have
to rebuild the whole product.

00:22:17.090 --> 00:22:18.947
They've been mixed actually.

00:22:18.947 --> 00:22:20.278
We've had some that were super successful.

00:22:20.278 --> 00:22:23.470
Twitch did a big redesign of our
mobile app, maybe three years ago,

00:22:23.470 --> 00:22:26.550
from the first version that had
basically been a cloned version of

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:28.857
the Justin.tv app to
a completely new version.

00:22:28.857 --> 00:22:31.417
It's basically the version
you can use today.

00:22:31.417 --> 00:22:38.064
And that increased engagement by 35,
40% per customer when we launched it.

00:22:38.064 --> 00:22:42.068
It's huge, changes that make that big
of an impact are actually really rare.

00:22:42.068 --> 00:22:43.744
&gt;&gt; Usually data errors.

00:22:43.744 --> 00:22:45.487
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, [LAUGH] yeah,
they're usually data errors.

00:22:45.487 --> 00:22:46.478
And in this case it wasn't.

00:22:46.478 --> 00:22:50.409
In this case,
it actually was a huge increase in usage.

00:22:50.409 --> 00:22:54.395
And so absolutely,
redesigns can make a big difference.

00:22:54.395 --> 00:22:58.092
But we weren't doing the big Twitch,
we didn't do that redesign because we'd

00:22:58.092 --> 00:23:01.307
discovered we couldn't move
forward on our existing product.

00:23:01.307 --> 00:23:04.470
Since we got some insights is
the what what things should

00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:06.637
be presented at the top level of a nav.

00:23:06.637 --> 00:23:09.602
And we re-did the navigation to present
those things at the top level instead of

00:23:09.602 --> 00:23:10.849
burying them three clicks deep.

00:23:10.849 --> 00:23:13.559
Three touches deep, I should say.

00:23:13.559 --> 00:23:18.435
Most of the time when I see people doing
a big redesign it's because they fall into

00:23:18.435 --> 00:23:22.319
this trap that engineers and
project managers often fall into.

00:23:22.319 --> 00:23:25.438
Which is,
we have to fix this eight things.

00:23:25.438 --> 00:23:28.619
Let's just fix them all
at once it'll be easier.

00:23:28.619 --> 00:23:32.838
That's wrong, you should just fix
them each one at a time individually.

00:23:32.838 --> 00:23:36.072
If you have eight things that are wrong
with your product that's awesome,

00:23:36.072 --> 00:23:37.827
you have this huge great list of things.

00:23:37.827 --> 00:23:40.723
Just fix them one at a time,
fix everything one at a time.

00:23:40.723 --> 00:23:41.993
Crank them out quickly,

00:23:41.993 --> 00:23:45.993
you'll get there way faster one change at
a time than you will in a big rewrite.

00:23:48.413 --> 00:23:49.174
That'd be great for

00:23:49.174 --> 00:23:52.057
a really early stage startup is almost
equivalent to pivoting the company.

00:23:52.057 --> 00:23:54.111
And we generally advise against it.

00:23:54.111 --> 00:23:58.590
&gt;&gt; It's this weird sort of
programmer product procrastination.

00:23:58.590 --> 00:23:59.651
If I don't know what to do,

00:23:59.651 --> 00:24:01.877
therefore I'm going to invent
this big project to work on.

00:24:01.877 --> 00:24:05.661
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We don't need to use that library.

00:24:05.661 --> 00:24:06.598
I'm going to build it myself.

00:24:06.598 --> 00:24:09.464
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Very common, use the damn library.

00:24:09.464 --> 00:24:14.274
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I'd like to talk

00:24:14.274 --> 00:24:18.565
a little bit about decision making.

00:24:18.565 --> 00:24:23.220
So maybe after you talk to your users but
before you decide what to build, usually

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:27.467
there has to be some type of consensus
building process either function or

00:24:27.467 --> 00:24:30.030
dysfunctional between the co-founders.

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:36.450
And what are some tips or
tricks on how to come to a consensus?

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:38.800
And then maybe more importantly,

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:42.170
how do you deal with the contrarian,
how do you deal with

00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:47.310
having to make a decision where you
know there's going to be a disagreement?

00:24:47.310 --> 00:24:49.340
&gt;&gt; I think the first thing you
said is actually super important.

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:50.800
It's something I want to reinforce.

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:55.430
You talk to your users first, and
then you have the ideas about the product.

00:24:55.430 --> 00:24:57.340
Almost everyone does it
in the opposite order.

00:24:57.340 --> 00:24:59.080
It's called product validation.

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:01.850
If you find yourself thinking, I'm going
to go talk to my users to validate my

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:06.530
product idea,
you've gone horribly off the rails.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:09.510
You do it in the other order, you don't
talk to users to validate your product

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:12.752
ideas, you talk to your users
to have your product ideas.

00:25:12.752 --> 00:25:16.540
And so
I would just have a rule in general for

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:20.670
people who want to have
a voice in product design,

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:23.266
is that they better talk to the users and
look at the data too.

00:25:23.266 --> 00:25:25.010
If you haven't talked to users and

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:29.860
you haven't looked at data, you don't get
to have an opinion about the product.

00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:33.140
I'm sorry, the person who's actually
done the work gets to have the opinion.

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:35.990
You can have ideas but
they get to make the call.

00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:40.054
But in case you wind up where both
people really have talked to the users.

00:25:40.054 --> 00:25:42.340
And both people really
have looked at the data.

00:25:42.340 --> 00:25:46.340
And people still disagree
as to what we should build.

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:48.320
Which by the way is
actually much more rare.

00:25:48.320 --> 00:25:50.350
Once both people have
actually talked to users and

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:54.290
actually looked at the data, it's usually
relatively easy to get to consensus.

00:25:54.290 --> 00:25:57.110
But it will come up where
people still disagree.

00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:03.190
I've found it's better when you
just have someone who's in charge.

00:26:03.190 --> 00:26:05.448
And at the end of the day,
you have someone who's the CEO, and

00:26:05.448 --> 00:26:08.420
everyone agrees this person's
judgement will be trusted.

00:26:08.420 --> 00:26:11.160
And you let them make the call.

00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:13.200
Don't avoid conflict.

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:14.040
Talk about it.
Argue about it,

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:19.030
but then you have someone who just gets
to make the call, because anything else

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:22.935
leads to just decision making
that's far too slow for a startup.

00:26:24.510 --> 00:26:27.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah, maybe it's because
I'm usually the one in charge,

00:26:27.930 --> 00:26:30.125
I just feel like I don't
have a lot of disagreements.

00:26:30.125 --> 00:26:31.938
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:26:31.938 --> 00:26:32.589
&gt;&gt; There are-

00:26:32.589 --> 00:26:34.070
&gt;&gt; Meet the CEO.

00:26:34.070 --> 00:26:34.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:26:36.770 --> 00:26:40.230
Largely, I find the situation I'm in,
I say one of two things a lot,

00:26:40.230 --> 00:26:44.820
which is I don't want to argue
about it if we can just test it,

00:26:46.190 --> 00:26:50.050
and I'm very willing to
be surprised on this.

00:26:50.050 --> 00:26:51.514
I'm probably right, but

00:26:51.514 --> 00:26:55.848
this isn't getting into my top three
important things to worry about today.

00:26:55.848 --> 00:26:58.825
So let's just see.

00:26:58.825 --> 00:27:03.340
It's easy to do that when you have
more resources, to let's just see.

00:27:03.340 --> 00:27:05.768
But I think about,
like Ciss and I over the years.

00:27:05.768 --> 00:27:09.123
And Adam and I over the years at Hipmunk,
I think as it panned out,

00:27:09.123 --> 00:27:13.272
we've very rarely had these philosophical
differences over with the product,

00:27:13.272 --> 00:27:14.625
sometimes it was timing.

00:27:14.625 --> 00:27:15.809
Should we build this thing now?

00:27:15.809 --> 00:27:18.230
What's the prioritization of these things?

00:27:18.230 --> 00:27:22.250
But we always agreed on general
mechanics of what we were doing and

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:23.705
why we were doing it.

00:27:23.705 --> 00:27:30.055
And so the strategy might change but the
details, we're very rarely not emotional.

00:27:30.055 --> 00:27:31.380
So those are getting emotional,

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:33.945
I think there's probably
something else structurally wrong.

00:27:33.945 --> 00:27:35.805
You might be swimming upstream.

00:27:35.805 --> 00:27:37.075
That happens a lot.

00:27:37.075 --> 00:27:41.392
The arguments have gotten that rid over
the last year like when we try to promote,

00:27:41.392 --> 00:27:45.605
we do cross promotion from mobile web
to the app, it just doesn't feel great.

00:27:45.605 --> 00:27:46.657
It works.

00:27:46.657 --> 00:27:49.815
It works and
we like the numbers going up but

00:27:49.815 --> 00:27:55.016
it's not really with the user's best
intention in mind all the time.

00:27:55.016 --> 00:27:57.446
Sometimes it drifts into this like
it's good for the company but

00:27:57.446 --> 00:27:58.700
maybe not for the users.

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:00.070
And when you're in those situations,

00:28:00.070 --> 00:28:02.420
that's usually when
the disagreements come out.

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:03.858
And you're not really arguing
about the future anymore.

00:28:03.858 --> 00:28:07.774
You're arguing about short term or long
term, or best for the business or best for

00:28:07.774 --> 00:28:08.540
the users.

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:09.960
And then when you beat at it,
and beat at it, and

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:13.160
beat at it, eventually you come to a
solution that actually feels right, right.

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:16.700
That meets your intuition, you don't feel
like you're fighting the technology,

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:18.210
you're not fighting the users anymore.

00:28:18.210 --> 00:28:19.655
Those are usually
situations I look out for.

00:28:19.655 --> 00:28:25.640
&gt;&gt; Sir, I want to get to you
guys telling product story but

00:28:25.640 --> 00:28:31.980
I want to cover one topic first which
is that lets take people passed lunch.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:36.960
Well now you need to have some type
of process around talking to users,

00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:42.560
looking at data, making a decision,
building a product, releasing a product.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:46.060
Checking your success and repeating,
product development cycle.

00:28:48.840 --> 00:28:53.100
What are some advice you can give to early
stage startups around how to set that up,

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:56.600
what that cadence should be,
maybe some pitfalls to avoid?

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:04.940
&gt;&gt; The first, just to take one
little step back is, I see a lot

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:09.160
of startups, they get too emotionally
high when the numbers are going up.

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:11.940
And they're not investing
in this sort of process.

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:13.950
Because growth masks all problems.

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:15.690
And then at some point
that kind of stops and

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:17.630
you don't have any of these mannerisms.

00:29:17.630 --> 00:29:20.662
I also see startups get a little too low
when things not going right at first.

00:29:20.662 --> 00:29:22.710
And they kind of psych themselves out.

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:24.475
So you want to live in that in between,
right?

00:29:24.475 --> 00:29:26.660
If the data's really good,
well, check the data.

00:29:26.660 --> 00:29:29.139
Right, if the data's really bad,
check the data.

00:29:29.139 --> 00:29:30.179
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:30.179 --> 00:29:31.282
&gt;&gt; Okay, so then in terms

00:29:31.282 --> 00:29:35.234
of the actual process, first of all it's
going to be different at every company and

00:29:35.234 --> 00:29:37.620
it's going to change, as you scale.

00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:43.845
I think it's just most important
to have a way of working,

00:29:43.845 --> 00:29:50.717
to have if you just want to start
out really, really specific of.

00:29:50.717 --> 00:29:53.400
The way I like to think about it is
where do we want to be in a year?

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:56.810
Okay, let's draw a straight
line from that to here,

00:29:56.810 --> 00:29:59.330
what do we need to be doing
today to get there in a year?

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:00.930
So that's why I like our hypothesis.

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:03.600
Do we need to validate
anything about that?

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:05.240
Probably a few things here or there.

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:07.300
Do that, go to check out.

00:30:07.300 --> 00:30:09.930
Yes, and
then it's just like straight line.

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:11.370
And I like to work on two week cycles.

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:14.680
Check in every week on
basically that kind of thing.

00:30:14.680 --> 00:30:15.470
Where do we want to be in a year?

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:16.950
Are we still on track,
are we still doing the right thing?

00:30:16.950 --> 00:30:18.550
Does it still make sense?

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:20.480
Are our assumptions still
holding to be true?

00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:23.630
If not, pop it up a stack.

00:30:23.630 --> 00:30:27.030
And then you give people whatever it is.

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:29.690
If you're early startup,
you give them 13 days to work.

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:32.539
And if you're a more mature startup,
you give them nine days to work.

00:30:32.539 --> 00:30:33.685
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:30:33.685 --> 00:30:35.180
&gt;&gt; And then keep going on at that.

00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:38.210
I found that that's worked really well for
me.

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:42.790
&gt;&gt; One thing I found actually is that
if your goal of talking to users

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:48.110
shouldn't be course corrections
on a week to week basis.

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:52.430
I did a whole crap on a talking
users at the very start of

00:30:52.430 --> 00:30:56.850
trying to figure out what the users
wanted, how to think about them.

00:30:56.850 --> 00:31:02.900
But then actually, we didn't because we
had other reasons to go talk to users,

00:31:02.900 --> 00:31:05.530
but we could have gone six months
without talking to a user,

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:07.000
because actually we
knew what users wanted.

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:08.885
They wanted to make money on the service.

00:31:08.885 --> 00:31:13.212
Streamers wanted to be able to make money,
they wanted to reach more viewers and

00:31:13.212 --> 00:31:16.714
they wanted positive social feedback for
what they were doing.

00:31:16.714 --> 00:31:18.932
And you kind of just check
anything you were doing,

00:31:18.932 --> 00:31:21.980
is this going to deliver this
to our streamers, yes or no?

00:31:21.980 --> 00:31:23.650
And if so how much?

00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:24.775
Great, you don't have to talk to anybody.

00:31:24.775 --> 00:31:27.380
You already know what they want.

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:31.260
And you need to go back and
revisit it and sort of talk to users

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:35.050
periodically because you need to
understand more of the nuances.

00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:39.802
The things about your service that are
bugging them [INAUDIBLE] doing something

00:31:39.802 --> 00:31:40.666
really cool.

00:31:40.666 --> 00:31:44.225
But the goal of talking to users is not
to get them to tell you what features to

00:31:44.225 --> 00:31:46.330
build because users
are really bad at that.

00:31:46.330 --> 00:31:48.345
They actually have no idea
what features to build.

00:31:48.345 --> 00:31:51.350
[INAUDIBLE] users is to get to
understand them really well.

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:53.450
And users don't change that fast.

00:31:53.450 --> 00:31:59.360
So I personally prefer more
in-depth time With my customers,

00:31:59.360 --> 00:32:00.743
talking to them for a while.

00:32:00.743 --> 00:32:04.405
And then, depending on the size of
the thing we need to do or we're trying to

00:32:04.405 --> 00:32:09.160
move, we might go just work and build and
look at metrics for the next six months.

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:12.740
And not actually talk to
users that much for a while.

00:32:12.740 --> 00:32:16.780
Because if you know that what
people want is to make more money,

00:32:16.780 --> 00:32:21.480
you can spend six months optimizing
the sell through rate on an object or

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:23.230
on how much advertising you have.

00:32:23.230 --> 00:32:29.485
And you just can have faith that it's
going to be good and you will be rewarded.

00:32:29.485 --> 00:32:32.011
&gt;&gt; I think the classic advice
that's very difficult to follow is,

00:32:32.011 --> 00:32:34.845
you need to have the courage
of your convictions.

00:32:34.845 --> 00:32:38.955
So, you decide what you're going to build,
it might take six months to build it.

00:32:38.955 --> 00:32:41.079
And at Reddit we just kind of
do it in front of everybody and

00:32:41.079 --> 00:32:43.082
get flamed by users the whole time.

00:32:43.082 --> 00:32:46.700
Which is fine, I'm used to it, but I have
to kind of tell our product people like,

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:48.550
just keep pushing, keep pushing.

00:32:48.550 --> 00:32:52.750
Just ship a little bit every week, and
then in six months, they'll get it.

00:32:52.750 --> 00:32:53.870
And at the same time,

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:56.750
the completely contradictory advice is
you have to know when you're wrong.

00:32:56.750 --> 00:32:58.190
You have to be able to
identify when you're wrong.

00:32:58.190 --> 00:33:02.670
You have to be able to say, all of
you people are wrong, and I'm right.

00:33:02.670 --> 00:33:05.575
And then at some point you have to decide,
wait no, you're right.

00:33:05.575 --> 00:33:11.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And if you can walk that line, you need

00:33:11.130 --> 00:33:16.410
to have this like good combination of high
ego and high humility and balance those.

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:18.310
But I always try to just kind
of run this process in my head.

00:33:18.310 --> 00:33:19.505
Wait, am I wrong?

00:33:19.505 --> 00:33:22.694
Nope, they're still wrong.

00:33:22.694 --> 00:33:26.250
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; As much as I said don't talk to users,

00:33:26.250 --> 00:33:29.440
you don't have to talk to users every
week, it's totally unnecessary.

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:33.600
You do need to look at your numbers
every week, you really need to know.

00:33:33.600 --> 00:33:35.985
And you don't need to look at them every
day, it's actually like pointless and

00:33:35.985 --> 00:33:37.955
almost just ego gratification to
look at your numbers every day.

00:33:37.955 --> 00:33:41.732
But you should look at your
numbers every single week, and

00:33:41.732 --> 00:33:45.831
you should confront, are they up or
down and ask yourself why.

00:33:45.831 --> 00:33:51.008
And the holy grail of product design
is for your numbers to move and

00:33:51.008 --> 00:33:54.505
for you to truly
understand why they moved.

00:33:56.650 --> 00:33:57.920
We still struggle with that.

00:33:57.920 --> 00:34:02.910
Like, there have been points where
I feel like I understood it, and

00:34:02.910 --> 00:34:05.550
then it slips away again as
the business gets more complicated.

00:34:05.550 --> 00:34:10.220
But you're always trying to be able to
explain fully down to like atomic ground

00:34:10.220 --> 00:34:18.320
truth, we are up 20% this week because
we got this many more new devices in.

00:34:18.320 --> 00:34:21.150
And our retention rate is
this on those devices, and

00:34:21.150 --> 00:34:22.160
this is the conversation rates.

00:34:22.160 --> 00:34:26.510
And if you do the math and you can explain
it all the way down to this new feature

00:34:26.510 --> 00:34:31.040
moved this conversation rate on this page,
by this amount, and therefore we are up.

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:33.850
And if you can really do that,
it's super powerful.

00:34:33.850 --> 00:34:34.960
It's also really hard.

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:38.276
But you'll never get there if you don't
build with the intention of looking at it

00:34:38.276 --> 00:34:38.830
every week.

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:43.370
&gt;&gt; So in the last five minutes
before we open for questions,

00:34:44.620 --> 00:34:49.280
can you guys walk through a feature
that is visible on your sites now?

00:34:50.620 --> 00:34:55.200
And I'll let you choose,
that's either bad or good.

00:34:55.200 --> 00:35:00.553
And talk about the process
of how it came to be.

00:35:03.671 --> 00:35:06.225
&gt;&gt; I have a lot.

00:35:06.225 --> 00:35:09.781
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'll talk about

00:35:09.781 --> 00:35:12.420
one of my favorite ones.

00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:13.639
This is from the early days of Reddit.

00:35:13.639 --> 00:35:18.300
So the early days of Reddit,
it was just links.

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:19.210
Everything was external.

00:35:20.260 --> 00:35:25.460
And eventually we added comments,
probably about six months in.

00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:27.485
The first comment was,
this is the end of Reddit.

00:35:27.485 --> 00:35:29.450
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The first of,

00:35:29.450 --> 00:35:33.100
that would be the first comment on
every product we've ever released.

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:40.350
And users started doing this
curious thing where they would, so

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:42.550
on any post you could go,
you could click on the link or

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:47.040
you could click on the comments page
to go to the comments for that page.

00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:50.640
Our IDs for posts are just
a counter that's in the URL.

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:55.230
So users would submit the URL that
would represent the comment page for

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:56.730
the URL they're submitting, right?

00:35:56.730 --> 00:35:58.800
So that would just increment,
they would take the newest post and

00:35:58.800 --> 00:36:00.830
increment by one and submit that URL.

00:36:00.830 --> 00:36:03.270
So that you had a post that would
link to its own comments page.

00:36:05.590 --> 00:36:08.020
And it was really cool, so
we called those self posts.

00:36:08.020 --> 00:36:10.170
And users were doing this, and
I remember watching them do it thinking,

00:36:10.170 --> 00:36:10.760
this is really cool.

00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:12.590
I would have never thought to do this.

00:36:12.590 --> 00:36:14.610
Sometimes they'd guess wrong, right?

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:17.865
And so they'd submit a post to
somebody else's comment page.

00:36:17.865 --> 00:36:19.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Which was funny.

00:36:19.420 --> 00:36:24.523
And so because our user base at the time
was fairly technical, the way we made this

00:36:24.523 --> 00:36:30.780
work is when you're submitting a link, you
would just type in in the URL area, self.

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:33.390
And it would automatically do that
little circular thing for you.

00:36:33.390 --> 00:36:35.550
And that was the beginning
of self posts on Reddit.

00:36:37.010 --> 00:36:38.918
Self posts are now 60% of our content.

00:36:38.918 --> 00:36:41.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I don't know, a few million a day.

00:36:44.220 --> 00:36:49.820
And we would've never built that feature
if we hadn't been watching our users.

00:36:49.820 --> 00:36:53.980
I don't think I, I mean I'm
explaining it now, but if I explained

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:57.000
that feature without it existing I don't
know if it would make a ton of sense.

00:36:57.000 --> 00:37:00.300
And users, most of them didn't
get it at first either.

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:03.110
They were just like,
comments are destroying the site and

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:04.930
there's not even a link here,
it's just comments.

00:37:06.580 --> 00:37:11.580
And it just kind of goes to show, I think
the best product ideas are the ones

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:16.070
where your users doing it anyway,
and you just grease it a little bit.

00:37:16.070 --> 00:37:19.220
That's when I talk about before where
you're either swimming upstream, or

00:37:19.220 --> 00:37:21.160
just everything just feels right.

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:23.180
That was a classic example
of it just felt right.

00:37:23.180 --> 00:37:25.200
And it completely changed the site.

00:37:26.490 --> 00:37:30.970
&gt;&gt; I think my favorite story here
is an advertising product actually,

00:37:30.970 --> 00:37:32.250
which is funny.

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:37.670
So all of our users told us
really really clearly, we want to

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:43.930
show advertising and
make money off of our streams.

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:45.920
It was a clear desire,
everybody wanted it.

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:49.770
And they also told us their
least favorite thing about

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:53.960
our competitors' products was that they
would show these ads over the stream, and

00:37:53.960 --> 00:37:57.870
it would be disruptive to their
user's experience, and they hated it.

00:37:57.870 --> 00:38:01.160
And it was very interesting getting
these two pieces of feedback because

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:02.470
they both really wanted us to put ads on.

00:38:02.470 --> 00:38:04.870
And sometimes, literally,
sometimes different people,

00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:06.380
sometimes it was literally
the same people.

00:38:06.380 --> 00:38:08.630
Their biggest complaint about
the opposing service was,

00:38:09.860 --> 00:38:14.180
competitive service is annoying
my viewers with all these ads.

00:38:14.180 --> 00:38:17.480
And this was like,
this is a hard nut to crack.

00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:20.020
Because you kind of need to solve for
both,

00:38:20.020 --> 00:38:23.050
you're not allowed to just give up
as a product manager and pick one.

00:38:23.050 --> 00:38:25.980
And I'm really proud of our solution,
which was, we gave them a button.

00:38:25.980 --> 00:38:27.870
We inverted it, and

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:31.530
we gave the streamer the ability to hit
a button to run ads on their own channel.

00:38:32.995 --> 00:38:36.060
And, like it's funny because
normally you'd think

00:38:37.250 --> 00:38:41.000
of that as something that will, if you're
uploading content to like Facebook,

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:45.930
you would never press a button on that
to show more ads to your friends.

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:47.550
But because we were cutting
them in on the revenue,

00:38:47.550 --> 00:38:49.910
they were actually heavily
incentivized to hit this button.

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:52.880
And we basically got this paid workforce

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:57.480
of people placing advertising
in the downtime of the stream.

00:38:57.480 --> 00:38:58.950
And I'm really proud of
that clever solution and

00:38:58.950 --> 00:39:01.610
I think it points at something that I
think is a really important principle of

00:39:01.610 --> 00:39:02.380
product design in general.

00:39:02.380 --> 00:39:07.720
Which is, that you want to just try
inverting all of your assumptions.

00:39:07.720 --> 00:39:10.230
We got stuck on that
advertising problem for

00:39:10.230 --> 00:39:14.130
a long time because we didn't want to make
it disruptive to the customer experience.

00:39:14.130 --> 00:39:18.230
And we couldn't figure out how to automate
putting it on the stream in a good way.

00:39:18.230 --> 00:39:22.820
And at the same time we really wanted
to show ads on the video stream.

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:23.500
And we had

00:39:25.040 --> 00:39:30.630
this implicit assumption in our heads that
we had to figure out where to put the ad.

00:39:30.630 --> 00:39:32.610
And once we gave that assumption up,

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:34.670
it was much more clear
what to do going forward.

00:39:34.670 --> 00:39:37.719
And I think that's a, and
it's one of the most, I don't know,

00:39:37.719 --> 00:39:41.052
I'm just really proud of that product
today because it was one of the things

00:39:41.052 --> 00:39:43.056
that made our broadcasters super happy.

00:39:43.056 --> 00:39:46.090
And no one, like you can't complain,
you hit the button.

00:39:46.090 --> 00:39:49.140
&gt;&gt; How many times is that
button clicked every day now?

00:39:49.140 --> 00:39:51.476
You know, that's a really good question,
I don't know the numbers off hand, a lot,

00:39:51.476 --> 00:39:52.410
it's clicked very often.

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:54.424
People really like that
button because it's a,

00:39:54.424 --> 00:39:56.792
click the button make $15 button and
like, you know.

00:39:56.792 --> 00:40:00.796
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:40:00.796 --> 00:40:02.140
they're also aware of the trade off.

00:40:02.140 --> 00:40:05.340
Which is that their viewers don't like
it when they hit the button too much.

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:09.340
And so by putting that in their court,
they get to make the optimal trade-off for

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:11.810
their stream and their situation.

00:40:13.550 --> 00:40:17.000
&gt;&gt; All right, those are my questions,
I want to hook it up to you guys.

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:19.137
Happy to talk about product,
happy to talk about YC stuff,

00:40:19.137 --> 00:40:20.860
whatever you'd like to chat about,
go ahead.

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:25.410
&gt;&gt; What about the process of discovering
you've had product market fit.

00:40:25.410 --> 00:40:29.736
Getting past that launch pathway and
discovering there's something sustainable,

00:40:29.736 --> 00:40:33.290
beyond this real core group of users
that are really committed to you?

00:40:33.290 --> 00:40:37.442
&gt;&gt; Just to repeat the question, how did
you know when you had product market fit?

00:40:37.442 --> 00:40:43.640
So at Reddit, this is the nice scenario,
we were growing.

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:46.956
Despite not knowing anything
about our business,

00:40:46.956 --> 00:40:51.814
knowing little about our users,
not really knowing how to run a business,

00:40:51.814 --> 00:40:55.220
not having a ton of product sense,
being down a lot.

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:57.570
We were growing.

00:40:57.570 --> 00:41:00.840
So that's the nice scenario,
that's a good one.

00:41:00.840 --> 00:41:04.955
At Hipmunk,
our natural state was zero users.

00:41:04.955 --> 00:41:08.784
I'm not sure we ever
got product market fit.

00:41:08.784 --> 00:41:12.301
The users who came to Hipmunk liked it,
but

00:41:12.301 --> 00:41:16.345
we had to fight for
every single one of them.

00:41:16.345 --> 00:41:20.555
It's possible to do that, but we were,
again, I think that entire company,

00:41:20.555 --> 00:41:23.775
we were swimming upstream.

00:41:23.775 --> 00:41:26.775
And I remember the day we
kind of realized that.

00:41:26.775 --> 00:41:28.975
We ran the survey of like,
why do you use Hipmunk?

00:41:30.085 --> 00:41:32.920
And we put a lot of
effort into the product,

00:41:32.920 --> 00:41:34.330
we thought it was a really good product.

00:41:34.330 --> 00:41:38.435
Legitimately you can find a flight a lot
faster on us than our competitors.

00:41:38.435 --> 00:41:41.805
35% of people said it's
because of the logo, and

00:41:41.805 --> 00:41:46.565
50% of the people said because we
had lower prices, which we didn't.

00:41:46.565 --> 00:41:52.633
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, [LAUGH] I mean it's like,

00:41:52.633 --> 00:41:54.611
we made it.

00:41:54.611 --> 00:41:56.020
The company ran for five years.

00:41:56.020 --> 00:41:59.370
And I ended up selling,
which is fine, but yeah.

00:41:59.370 --> 00:42:01.364
I think we were really fighting that one.

00:42:01.364 --> 00:42:05.230
&gt;&gt; I think this is a huge misconception,
by the way.

00:42:05.230 --> 00:42:08.460
Most of the companies that apply to
YC don't have product market fit.

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:11.400
Most of the companies on demo day
don't have product market fit.

00:42:11.400 --> 00:42:13.550
Most companies die before
product market fit.

00:42:15.580 --> 00:42:20.140
A lot of people talk about product market
fit as this organic step in the process.

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:22.220
You raise angel,
then you get an office, and

00:42:22.220 --> 00:42:24.610
you hire some people, and
you have product market fit.

00:42:24.610 --> 00:42:29.070
And it's just like, you would be
surprised at how few companies.

00:42:29.070 --> 00:42:34.350
Product market fit is typically described
as, you are overwhelmed with demand for

00:42:34.350 --> 00:42:35.600
your product.

00:42:35.600 --> 00:42:38.150
&gt;&gt; It's really obvious when
you've hit product market fit.

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:42.070
So your product is bad, you can point at
seven things that are broken about it.

00:42:43.920 --> 00:42:48.140
And you have all these features
you know you need to build.

00:42:48.140 --> 00:42:51.200
And weirdly,
it's growing really fast anyways.

00:42:51.200 --> 00:42:52.920
It's really obvious when you
have product market fit,

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:54.588
your product is growing like crazy.

00:42:54.588 --> 00:42:57.920
And you are spending, you don't get to
fix any of the things that are broken or

00:42:57.920 --> 00:42:58.565
bad about your product,

00:42:58.565 --> 00:43:03.300
because you're spending all of
your time fixing it as it scales.

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:06.877
And you are, so the metaphor I use for
startups is kind of Sisyphean,

00:43:06.877 --> 00:43:08.987
you're pushing this boulder up a hill.

00:43:08.987 --> 00:43:10.799
And you're pushing the boulder and

00:43:10.799 --> 00:43:13.948
you're pushing the boulder,
you're pushing the boulder.

00:43:13.948 --> 00:43:16.020
Product market fit is
when you crest the hill.

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:20.710
And your job doesn't get easier, but
it does change a lot, because now you

00:43:20.710 --> 00:43:24.398
are chasing the boulder as it tries to
roll away from you down the other side.

00:43:24.398 --> 00:43:28.830
And now you're springing flat out, trying
to catch up, which is hard in its own way.

00:43:28.830 --> 00:43:33.157
But it feels totally different, because
as whereas before you have this, God,

00:43:33.157 --> 00:43:37.060
where every inch is like,
as Steve was describing with Hipmunk,

00:43:37.060 --> 00:43:40.610
every inch is you're pushing this boulder
and you're fighting for every inch.

00:43:40.610 --> 00:43:45.990
Now it's like, crap, if we don't keep up
with this, it's going to run away from us.

00:43:45.990 --> 00:43:48.330
And that's a totally different feeling,
and you'll know.

00:43:48.330 --> 00:43:49.900
&gt;&gt; And here's the problem.

00:43:49.900 --> 00:43:52.790
Eventually you'll catch the boulder on
the way down, and then you're like ****,

00:43:52.790 --> 00:43:54.207
I'm actually pushing it uphill again.

00:43:54.207 --> 00:43:55.498
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yep, absolutely.

00:43:55.498 --> 00:43:57.485
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's what happens.

00:43:57.485 --> 00:44:01.123
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I guess it's like asking,

00:44:01.123 --> 00:44:03.654
how do you know if you're in love?

00:44:03.654 --> 00:44:06.363
Trust me, you know.

00:44:06.363 --> 00:44:11.695
&gt;&gt; It's funny, because I think one of the
major problems that YC companies have is,

00:44:11.695 --> 00:44:16.250
how do they stay small and lean and
don't spend a lot of money.

00:44:16.250 --> 00:44:17.490
Don't have a management overhead,

00:44:17.490 --> 00:44:21.780
don't have a lot of bills,
until they reach product market.fit.

00:44:21.780 --> 00:44:26.040
One of the typical mistakes is
confusing an angel round or

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:27.990
a couple million dollars in the bank for
product market fit.

00:44:29.600 --> 00:44:31.556
It's extremely common.

00:44:31.556 --> 00:44:32.854
All right, other questions?

00:44:35.360 --> 00:44:38.887
&gt;&gt; How do you recognize
your first group of user?

00:44:41.470 --> 00:44:44.720
&gt;&gt; How do you recognize
your first groups of users?

00:44:44.720 --> 00:44:48.280
&gt;&gt; Reddit was easy,
they were just like Alexis and I.

00:44:49.790 --> 00:44:53.970
Emmet was kind of talking about
the intuition phase of building products.

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:55.890
We were in that for a long time.

00:44:55.890 --> 00:44:58.880
Our users, they read the same
Paul Graham blog that we did.

00:44:59.990 --> 00:45:03.268
They were programmers like I was,
so it was really, really easy.

00:45:03.268 --> 00:45:09.837
Hipmunk, we were actually completely
wrong about who our users were.

00:45:09.837 --> 00:45:11.750
And I don't know if we ever
quite solved this problem.

00:45:11.750 --> 00:45:12.780
They solved it through acquisition.

00:45:12.780 --> 00:45:18.500
Which is, we built a product for
ourselves, like tech savvy

00:45:18.500 --> 00:45:24.290
people who travel a fair amount and
buy their own travel.

00:45:24.290 --> 00:45:28.330
But in reality, the only way to make
money in travel is to sell to business

00:45:28.330 --> 00:45:31.090
travelers, because they're the only one
that travels multiple times a year.

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:35.115
And business travelers don't get use
things like Hipmunk or Kayak, right?

00:45:35.115 --> 00:45:36.980
They use American Express and Concur.

00:45:36.980 --> 00:45:40.129
So it took us actually a couple
years to synthesize that, and

00:45:40.129 --> 00:45:41.504
we were just like, ****.

00:45:41.504 --> 00:45:48.809
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think there's two kinds of startups.

00:45:48.809 --> 00:45:52.279
There are startups that
are building things for themselves,

00:45:52.279 --> 00:45:54.621
where your customer is easy to identify.

00:45:54.621 --> 00:45:56.684
It's you and people like you.

00:45:56.684 --> 00:46:00.606
And there are startups building things for
third party group users.

00:46:00.606 --> 00:46:05.492
Where you should arrive at which
customers are your customers through

00:46:05.492 --> 00:46:07.410
an analytic mode, right?

00:46:07.410 --> 00:46:09.080
Through like thinking about the problem.

00:46:09.080 --> 00:46:10.960
So Twitch was an analytic start up,

00:46:10.960 --> 00:46:14.190
because I was a huge fan
of watching game streaming.

00:46:14.190 --> 00:46:18.350
But actually I wasn't much of a streamer,
I've never been much of a streamer.

00:46:18.350 --> 00:46:23.506
And I basically dug in on Justin TV and
realized that 80% of

00:46:23.506 --> 00:46:28.979
the minutes watched were coming
from our top 200 streamers.

00:46:28.979 --> 00:46:31.240
And I was like they're
the most important people.

00:46:31.240 --> 00:46:33.540
We lose them, we lose everything,
and if we can get 200 more of them,

00:46:33.540 --> 00:46:34.306
we'll be twice as big.

00:46:34.306 --> 00:46:39.250
And so it was sort of
identified analytically that

00:46:39.250 --> 00:46:42.990
that was the right group of users,
and those are the right customers.

00:46:42.990 --> 00:46:46.920
And I think that you should
just know which one you are.

00:46:46.920 --> 00:46:51.550
And the most important thing there is,
if it's not for you, if you're not

00:46:51.550 --> 00:46:56.546
legitimately building something for you
and for you to use, and you aren't a big

00:46:56.546 --> 00:47:01.281
group of people in this case, you should
absolutely use the analytic mode.

00:47:01.281 --> 00:47:03.160
I don't know, it's different for
every single company.

00:47:03.160 --> 00:47:05.730
But think hard about it, I don't know.

00:47:05.730 --> 00:47:08.604
&gt;&gt; But if it's not for you,
your intuition is lying to you.

00:47:08.604 --> 00:47:09.376
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yes, yes.

00:47:09.376 --> 00:47:16.091
&gt;&gt; Every time, and you have to supplement
with data or talking to users or whatever.

00:47:16.091 --> 00:47:20.130
&gt;&gt; So shockingly, we were 100% on time.

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:22.702
If everyone can thank these guys for
coming out today, appreciate it.

00:47:22.702 --> 00:47:26.300
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

